A||N||18||fropuff||HELP!  What can I use???||||1||
Z||000000||fropuff||10-08-2000||10:16 PM||||Hey, I am thinking of fiberglassing one of rockcrawl's scoops to a plastic quarter panel from 2M4 Dale.  Rockcrawl said fiberglass resin won't hold.  The scoop is fiberglass?  and the panel is plastic.  What can I use?  I want it to be flexible so it doesn't crack too.  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Philphine||10-08-2000||10:30 PM||||i have'nt glassed to this plastic but to plastic in general i think it will hold. probaly have to sand it to get to raw (?) plastic. i think the problem would be that they wouldn't flex the same. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||fropuff||10-08-2000||10:37 PM||||The way I'm using it, I don't really want it to "hold".  The way I'm doing it, I'm putting it on through the back of the panel.  All I want to do is fill in all the cracks, and help hold it a little.  I also don't want it to crack.  I will sand it down to the plastic if needed, and I hope it will work.  Is there a flexible kind of resin that I can use?  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cooter||10-08-2000||11:17 PM||||You could some filler made for flexible bumper covers. It comes in small tubes and mixes like JB-Weld. It kind of smells like JB weld also. Another probability would be to use some SMC adhesive/filler. It is like fiberglass resin/thin body filler and is made to glue composite panels together with. I used it to bond my hood scoop on with and it stuck really good to everything! (Hands, headlight doors, bumper cover and on the floor where it dripped)||||reg||1
Z||000004||BAM-BAM||10-09-2000||12:32 AM||||   Fropuff, why not get ahold of rockcrawl and find out how he did it? That would seem to me to be the easiest way. His were afixed somehow.<P>  Later, BAM OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT<BR>Eibach Springs/KYB shocks and struts<BR>Mr.Mikes 500 series seats, Carpet and headliner! YEAH BABY-YEAH||||reg||12
Z||000005||fropuff||10-09-2000||07:54 AM||||Thanks everyone.  I think I will get some of the crap Cooter told me about.  The way that rockcrawl's were on were by adhesive tape.  I don't think he glassed any in yet.  Thanks again for the quick help.  I needed to know so I can order a quarter panel from 2M4 Dale.||||reg||1
Z||000006||rogergarrison||10-10-2000||10:01 AM||||the 2 tube bumper repair stuff is 3M#5895. make sure you get all the paint off where you use it. IT WILL NOT STICK TO ANY PAINT !! Also dont believe the label, let it harden overnite. Can be sanded with #80 and finer, then primer. The stuff is great, ive put destroyed bumper covers back together again with it. It stays very flexible.||||reg||1
Z||000007||wla21841||10-10-2000||08:55 PM||||Also 3M 8101 will do the job. It is used for the flex panel repair also. $15.49 locally.<P>Bill||||reg||1
Z||000008||contra||10-11-2000||10:54 AM||||All you need to do remove the paint on the panel<BR>scuff up the spot where the scoop is going to go pretty good<BR>then glass it in. i'm pretty sure you HAVE to glass it in, i dont think anything else will hold well. If you're not sure you could use fiberglass fibers as well, they have some flex to them.<P>that's how i glassed my bumper addon to my fascia, it flexes fery little, but it's still good!||||reg||1
Z||000009||fropuff||12-07-2000||08:45 PM||||Okay, I decided to make my own side scoop.  It's kind of like rockcrawl's, but larger.  I am wondering what I can use to make the scoop itself.  Fiberglass cloth, resin, and Bondo??  How would Bondo stick, and would it bust off?  I am wondering if this really needs to flex.  How much does the panel actually flex anyway?  I am also wondering how I attach it to the panel.  Again, fiberglass and Bondo?  Any help is appreciated.  I'm going to make the scoop, adn probably sell it, so I want to get it right.  Thanks again. <P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||1
Z||000010||DaRkLoRD||12-08-2000||12:01 AM||||one of the problems with being at school, is I'm not at home.. I have a set of side scoops in progress back home.... which I'd also like to eventually sell..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||3
Z||000011||fropuff||12-13-2000||08:43 PM||||Okay, I decided to follow contra's advice (thanks!) and I glassed it on.  I put on a layer of cloth last week, and three more tonight.  I just went out, and it's about just as flexible as the panel itself.  Actually, the panel isn't very flexible anyway.  I might put one more layer on to be sure.  I am going to sand the edges smooth tomorrow w/ a palm sander, and I have another question.  I am wondering if I should use Bondo.  I checked on the 3M #05895, but the body shop was out of it.  I did also go on the 3M website and looked up the part number.  Yes, I do not have a life.  It said that the product cannot be used on fiberglass.  Of course, this is fiberglass.  I don't know what I should do about this.  Bondo?  Again, the panel isn't very flexible as you think, but I guess it might get a little shock from the road that might crack and crumble the Bondo, if I don't use something that is flexible.  By the way, I also don't want to spend a whole lot of money.  I have $55 to spend until Christmas.  Money sucks.  I need a job.  Anyway, I was wondering if someone could please help me out on this.  Thanks a lot.<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||1
Z||000012||fropuff||12-13-2000||09:32 PM||||I also forgot to say: I found on the 3M site a filler that is made for fiberglass and composite panels.  #05883 or something like that.  It is made for stiffer plastic.  Would this work?  Thanks again.<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||1
Z||000013||FieroJoe||12-13-2000||10:00 PM||||Bondo also has a line of body fillers just for fiberglass applications, I personally wouldnt even try to use the regular Bondo filler, it will most likely crack when used on a Fiero panel, when the panel flexes.<P>The Bondo fiberglass stuff are in blue cans, while the regular filler is in red.<P>------------------<BR>FieroJoe<BR>c311t@yahoo.com<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||rogergarrison||12-14-2000||02:24 PM||||when they say 'flexible' part, that dont necessarily mean bendable. A lot has to do with the expansion and shrinkage of material. (ie) if you put a thin glass part on a very thick plastic panel when its exposed to hot sun, engine heat ect. one will expand more than the other, cracking it at joint.Polyester filler is ok to finish body work with as long as you keep depth to a minimum. You can really see what im talking about when someone bonds a glass scoop on the hood of like a camaro which is metal. Engine heat on hood will cause the edges to all crack open-may take a year-may take a week, but its going to sooner or later<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||fropuff||12-14-2000||04:38 PM||||Thanks for the replies.  So what should I use?  I won't get it until Saturday, so I don't need to know right away.  Thanks.<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||1
Z||000016||jerpro||12-14-2000||06:17 PM||||ok....<BR>The materials you are talking about have different body masses, thus they expand and contract different and at different rates. ALso flexing is limited when two different elements are tried to be combined. One element by itself will flex easier and will last onger thatn two different elements combined.<BR>This will prove very frustrating when you install the scoop and after a couple days the scoop starts to crack. The cracks will not be very noticeable but will get worse with time.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>I would suggest to create a scoop with one composite alone which will not crack as easily, be of greater quality, and have it's fullest flexing properties.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||fropuff||12-14-2000||06:28 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000018||fropuff||12-14-2000||08:07 PM||||I do kind of had a hard time thinking that this isn't going to work.  The fiberglass seems very strong, and still flexes a little.  Are you talking about the Bondo that will crack?  The seams seem very strong, and it flexes when the panel flexes.  I will try to find some crap that will flex, unless there's something I will look out for.  It seems like a waste to stop trying.  Thanks.<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||1
